 { The final concept that we'll be introducing is another one that, as far as I know, does not have an official name. We'll be referring to a square with these characteristics as a 'pseudo outpost'. As the name suggests, such squares are not technically outposts. However, in practice they function very similarly to an outpost. This is because a piece occupying that square cannot be attacked without major consequence. Let's illustrate what we mean with our first example. Here we can see that Black's knight on e4 is not on an outpost, since White can play the move 1.f3. However, this would create far more problems than it would solve! } 1. { -KEY- } f3 $4 Ng3 $19 { White cannot avoid getting checkmated on h1. While it's true that most situations will not be this extreme, it happens pretty frequently that a piece can land on a square where the opposing side cannot evict it without their position deteriorating. If you spot such a square, you can often build a positional strategy around it. Let's take a look at a few real-world examples. } 
