 { This first position shows a very common situation, reached via the Sicilian Defense. White's knight is under fire and they have two possibilities to choose from: Nc3-e2 or Nc3-d5. } 1. { -KEY- } Nd5 $1 { The knight lands on the central square. The key here is that while Black can play the move ...e6 at any moment, this move will almost always be undesirable due to the consequent weakening of the pawn on d6. } ( 1. Ne2 $2 { A significant mistake. Not only is White rejecting the superior d5 square, but they are also giving Black a tactical opportunity in the centre: } Nxe4 $1 2. fxe4 Bxe4 { White faces a very challenging defensive task. } ) 
a5 { Defending the pawn. } ( 1... Bxd5 { Exchanging the knight allows White to transform the structure in their favor with } 2. exd5 $16 { White will challenge Black's strong bishop next. The semi-open e-file creates attacking opportunities in the centre, while castling is also dangerous due to White's advanced kingside pawns. } ) 
2. g5 $1 { Continuing the offensive on the kingside. Now ...e6 is even harder to play for Black, since that would leave an outpost on the f6 square. } hxg5 3. Nxg5 ( 3. hxg5 $6 { would have kept control over the f6 square, but it would have come at the cost of self-pinning along the h-file. } ) 
e6 { Finally Black can no longer stand the invading knight, and agrees to weaken their structure in the hopes of pushing it back. } 4. Bb5+ $1 { Sensing the moment, White plays as energetically as possible. } Bc6 ( 4... Kf8 { is hopeless, since after } 5. Nf4 $18 { the d6 pawn is vulnerable, as well as there being threats such as Bxc5 followed by Nfxe6+, winning the queen. } ) 
5. Bxc5 $1 { White has calculated to the finish, seeing that both ...Bxb5 and ...dxc5 lead to a win by force. } Bxb5 ( 5... dxc5 $2 { With best play, White can win the game quickly here. } 6. Bxc6 Rxc6 7. Qf4 $1 Nh6 8. Nf6 $3 { A brilliant sacrificial move. } Qxf6 9. Qb8 Ke7 10. Qb7 Kf8 11. e5 $1 { All of White's work would have been ruined with the hasty capture of the rook on c6, due to ...Qb2# } Qxe5 12. Rd8 ) 
6. Qf4 Nh6 7. Bb6 { White plays energetically at every turn. } Qd7 8. Nf6+ $1 { After seven moves, the knight finally leaves the pseudo outpost. And with this move the game is effectively over, since Black's dark squares will now be beyond repair. } Bxf6 9. Qxf6 $18 { In addition to control over the dark squares, White has concrete threats like Qxh8+ and Bxa5. In the game, the move ...e6 unraveled Black's position. However, White's knight on d5 was too powerful to ignore. It's this dynamic, this tension between piece play and structure that positional players exploit when they spot a pseudo outpost. } 
