 { In this position, White has to decide which piece to recapture with on g2. Which would you choose? } 1. { -KEY- } Nxg2 $1 { Correct! White spots the route to the d5 pseudo outpost. From this square, the White knight will be difficult to evict since ...c6 would leave the d6 pawn permanently weak. } ( 1. Kxg2 $2 { If White insists that they wish to keep the knight on h4 in order to try to plant it on f5 in the future, they will quickly lose their advantage. For example: } Qe4 2. Kh2 g5 $1 3. Ng2 { Black's pawn structure does a great job of locking the White knight out of the game. } b5 $13 { Having taken care of the kingside threats, Black switches to active play on the queenside. The position is unclear, but probably easier to play for Black due to their greater piece activity. } ) 
f6 { A natural move, looking to take the sting out of White's bishop-queen battery along the a1-h8 diagonal. Now White has several tempting continuations, among them Ng2-f4. However, there is an even stronger approach. } ( 1... g5 { would stop the knight from moving towards the pseudo outpost, but it comes at the cost of weakening the kingside significantly. After } 2. Rd5 $16 { White has pressure on both flanks, with the a5 pawn under fire and the h3-h4 pawn break on the cards. } ) 
2. c5 $1 { A clever pawn sacrifice, with the goal of prying open the d-file as well as shutting out Black's bishop. } ( 2. Nf4 { The problem with this move is that, concretely, Black generates some counterplay after } Ne5 { when after } 3. Qe2 { Black can continue to pressure White with the move } Qe4 $13 { White still stands better here, but their hand is forced into playing Bxe5 to ease the pressure, which is a piece exchange that they would rather avoid. } ) 
dxc5 3. b5 { The point. Now Black's bishop on a7 is out of the game indefinitely. } Ne5 4. Bxe5 fxe5 { Black accepts an isolated pawn on e5 in order to stop Nf4 ideas. } ( 4... Rxe5 $2 { The lack of control of the f4 square coupled with the weakness of the back rank will make it impossible for Black to defend. } 5. Qa2 $1 Kh8 6. Rd7 $18 { White is slicing through the light squares, while Black's pieces remain terribly uncoordinated. } ) 
5. Rd7 { White has remained flexible, shifting from a pseudo-outpost strategy to a more dynamic approach. The strategy has paid off, with Black's passive bishop, damaged structure, and unsafe king all contributing to White's large advantage. } Bb6 $2 { A mistake, but the position was already beyond repair. } 6. Nh4 $18 { The light squares are now impossible to defend, and the game ends quickly. } Rbd8 7. Qb3+ Kh8 8. Rad1 Rxd7 9. Rxd7 Qg8 10. Rf7 Kh7 11. Qd5 $18 { White wins, as Black has no good defense against the threat of Qe4+ and Ng6+. This example illustrates the link between the various aspects of positional play. In the beginning White pursues the pseudo outpost on d5. Black is able to guard against this threat, but in the process loses control over other key areas of the board. In the end, an attack along the weak light squares proves decisive. } 
