 { So far we've looked at pseudo outposts in the centre of the board. However, a pseudo outpost can be even more deadly when it appears on the opponent's kingside. Let's now show a game where Black lost quickly because of their failure to identify the pseudo outpost and to anticipate White's maneuver towards it. If you wish to test yourself, you can pause here and try to identify the pseudo outpost that White will seek to target in the coming moves. } 1. { -KEY- } d3 { If you guessed the f5 square, you got it right. White plays a quiet move with a devilish idea: to set up the maneuver Nd2-f1-g3-f5. The reason the f5 square is a pseudo outpost is because Black can only cover it by playing ...g6, a move that will heavily weaken the dark squares around their king. } h6 $6 { With this move, Black shows that they have not spotted the dangerous idea. Now, the move ...g6 becomes even harder to play in good circumstances, since then the h6 pawn will be left undefended. } 2. Nd2 b5 $6 { Black remains unaware of the danger brewing on the kingside. } 3. Nf1 $1 Qd6 $2 { Black continues to play too casually. Not only can the knight no longer be stopped from landing on f5, now it will do so with a tempo against the queen! } ( { Black still had enough time to save the game. However, the position demanded that they pay full attention to the battle taking place around the f5 square. } 3... Nd7 $1 { Black opens up the scope of their bishop along the h4-d8 diagonal, which will prove very useful soon. } 4. Ng3 Kh7 $1 { The king steps out to h7 in order to defend the h6 pawn. } 5. Nf5 Bg5 $1 { We now see the point, by playing in this way Black has safeguarded the weak h6 pawn. And this was the pawn that would give them problems if they tried kicking the knight out with ...g6. } 6. a4 { White's kingside progress has been halted, so they turn their attention to the queenside. } Bxc1 7. Rexc1 g6 $1 { Finally Black is ready to push back the White knight. } 8. Ne3 Nc5 $11 { The position is roughly equal since White's dangerous positional plan has been stopped in its tracks. } ) 
4. Ng3 Rad8 $2 { The final mistake. } ( { The last chance to defend involved turning full attention to the invading knight on f5: } 4... Kh7 5. Nf5 Qe6 6. Qg3 Rg8 7. Qh4 { White continues to apply a lot of pressure on the kingside. } Bf8 $1 8. Bd2 Ne8 $1 9. a4 Nd6 $1 { After a lot of work, Black is finally in a position to challenge the knight. The better structure still leaves White with an edge, but the game continues. } ) 
5. Nf5 { The knight lands on the weak square in ideal circumstances. } Qe6 6. Qg3 { There are too many threats, so Black resigned. This maneuver commonly occurs in 1.e4 e5 positions, so if you play this with either color it is worth paying special attention to this example. Knowledge of the typical ideas here will win (or lose!) you many games. Now that we have introduced power and pseudo outposts, let's take a look at one more example to close out the chapter where both of these concepts arise at the same time! } 
