 { While power and pseudo outposts are usually independent of each other, their paths can cross on occasion. Let's show an example of this now. } 1... { -KEY- } Nc8 $1 { Black wants to play ...f5 and plant a knight on e4, where it will be defended by two pawns. } 2. a4 $2 { White fails to sense the danger and uses their move to grab some space on the queenside. } Nd6 $1 { Already Black is in command, as the plan of ...f5 and ...Ne4 can no longer be stopped. } 3. Qd3 f5 $1 { Preparing to place the knight on e4 next. } 4. Rad1 ( { The problem for White is that the e4 square is a pseudo outpost, since f2-f3 causes too great a weakening in the position. For example: } 4. Be2 Ne4 5. f3 $2 Ng3 ( { The tactical approach, with } 5. Rad1 c5 $1 { , is even stronger. } ) 
6. Bf1 Qd6 7. Rad1 f4 $19 { By playing f2-f3, White's dark squares were left too weak. Black now has a complete clamp on them with the possibility to maneuver their knight to the central outpost on e3 next. } ) 
Ne4 $17 { Under the cover of the e4 knight, Black's queen can soon collect the stranded e6 pawn. } 5. Bxe4 { White sees nothing better than to eliminate the powerful knight, but now Black's structure is improved. } fxe4 6. Qe2 Rf6 7. f3 { The only way to save the pawn. } exf3 8. gxf3 Qd6 9. Kg2 Raf8 $19 { White has avoided the loss of the pawn so far, but it has come at the cost of ruining their kingside pawn chain. The pressure against weak pawns, combined with the weakness of White's king, leaves Black with a winning position. I hope you've enjoyed the coverage of pseudo outposts and the chapter as a whole. Next up, we'll summarize the key takeaways of this chapter before moving on to the puzzle section. } 
